const schema = [{"@context":"https://schema.org","@type":"Service","name":"Clinical study report","serviceType":"Regulatory writing service","provider":{"@type":"Organization","name":"Pubrica","url":"https://pubrica.com/"},"url":"https://pubrica.com/services/medical-writing/regulatory-writing/clinical-study-report/","description":"Full, abbreviated and synoptic clinical study reports to ICH E3, with patient narratives, integrated summaries and Module 2.5 and 2.7 content.","areaServed":"Worldwide","audience":{"@type":"Audience","audienceType":"Researchers, PhD scholars, research institutions, medical writers and life-science teams"}},{"@context":"https://schema.org","@type":"FAQPage","mainEntity":[{"@type":"Question","name":"What do you need to start?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"The protocol with all amendments, the statistical analysis plan with its amendments, the TLFs or their shells, and the deviation log. If narratives are in scope, the counts of deaths, serious adverse events and discontinuations.\n\nThe amendments matter as much as the originals. A change made before unblinding and the same change made after it are read completely differently, and only the dates establish which is which."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Can you start before the database locks?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Yes, and it is usually the right sequence. Methods, background and much of the structure can be drafted from the protocol and the SAP while the data is still being cleaned.\n\nWhat cannot be drafted early is anything that depends on the result. A report with results written before lock is a report that will need its conclusions revisited, and reviewers can usually tell."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Full, abbreviated or synoptic — how do we decide?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"It depends on the role the study plays in the submission and on what your agency expects. Pivotal and registrational studies get a full E3 report; supportive and early-phase studies often do not.\n\nWhichever you choose, the justification for abbreviating belongs in the document. An abbreviated report with no stated basis for abbreviation invites the question."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How are narratives priced?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Per narrative, on complexity and on the quality of the source material. A narrative built from a clean, complete listing costs materially less than one assembled from scattered sources.\n\nNarrative count is the single largest source of estimate error in CSR work. Give us the counts at the quote stage and the number will hold."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Will the report match our house template?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Yes. We write to your template and style guide where you have one, and to the TransCelerate CSR template where you do not.\n\nIf you have neither, this is worth settling before the first report rather than the third, because a house style established by accident across three reports is expensive to retrofit."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Can you write the Module 2.5 and 2.7 content too?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Yes, and writing them with the CSRs is cheaper and safer than writing them afterwards. Where the underlying reports were written for reuse, the summaries are largely extraction.\n\nWritten separately by a different team, the common failure is two documents describing the same analysis in incompatible terms, which then has to be reconciled under submission pressure."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Do you also do the statistics?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"We can. Analysis against your locked dataset, TLF production and SAP authoring with the estimand framed under E9(R1) are all available, and ordering them with the writing removes a handover.\n\nWhat we do not do is own the clinical database. Build, validation, lock and custody sit with your data management function or your CRO — and we write from what they lock."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Is this device work too, or drugs only?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Both, but they are different documents. Device clinical investigations are reported under ISO 14155:2026 rather than ICH E3, and the report feeds the clinical evaluation rather than a CTD module.\n\nWorth noting that ISO 14155:2020 is withdrawn — the fourth edition replaced it on 23 March 2026 — so a device report template written before that needs checking."}}]},{"@context":"https://schema.org","@type":"BreadcrumbList","itemListElement":[{"@type":"ListItem","position":1,"name":"Home","item":"https://pubrica.com/"},{"@type":"ListItem","position":2,"name":"Services","item":"https://pubrica.com/services/"},{"@type":"ListItem","position":3,"name":"Regulatory writing","item":"https://pubrica.com/services/medical-writing/regulatory-writing/clinical-study-report/"}]}];

